**The open ground.**
- *Measured west of Pokrovsk* [src:osm-overpass-2026-landscape-sample]:
  - 67% of the 100 km² sample box is mapped as farmland;
  - fields have a median of 65 ha and median dimensions of 625 × 1,348 m;
  - shelterbelts run NNW–SSE and are under-mapped, at only 0.28 km per km² in OSM;
  - tracks run at 0.55 km per km², primary to tertiary roads at 0.13 km per km².
- *Settlements.* The same box holds 2,758 buildings in eight settlements [src:microsoft-global-ml-building-footprints], and villages sit along wooded balkas [src:eox-2021-s2cloudless].
- *Treelines.* A 1 km treeline near Pokrovsk was the unit of defence: an exposed 80 × 30 m trench in it was destroyed, while two concealed holes held for 10 days [src:wotr-2026-maurin-front-line].
- *Ravines.* The August infiltrators used ravines running north–south and the summer foliage [src:kyivindependent-2025-farrell-dobropillia-lessons].

**The outcome the player must reproduce:**
- **Ships sunk (uncontested, also counted by Oryx):**
  - *Ivanovets* (Project 1241.1/12411 Tarantul-III), 31 Jan – 1 Feb 2024, near Lake Donuzlav;
  - *Tsezar Kunikov* (Project 775 Ropucha-I), 14 Feb 2024, about 4 km off Alupka;
  - *Sergey Kotov* (Project 22160), 5 Mar 2024, near the Kerch Strait. A Russian insider places the wreck 5 km off Cape Takil [src:oryx-russian-equipment-losses][src:pravda-2024-yusov-kunikov-crew][src:isw-2024-03-05-roca].
- **Earlier damage to reproduce:**
  - *Admiral Makarov* and *Ivan Golubets* at Sevastopol (29 Oct 2022);
  - *Ivan Khurs* (24 May 2023);
  - *Olenegorsky Gornyak* holed at Novorossiysk, and the tanker *Sig* holed near Kerch (4 Aug 2023);
  - the Serna and Akula landing craft off Chornomorske (10 Nov 2023) [src:pravda-2022-geoconfirmed-admiral-makarov][src:pravda-2024-hur-ivan-khurs-details][src:pravda-2023-novorossiysk-olenegorsky-gornyak][src:shipandbunker-2023-sig-tanker][src:pravda-2024-romaniuk-magura-target-eliminate].
- **Aircraft (contested):**
  - *31 Dec 2024.* HUR first claimed one Mi-8 destroyed and one damaged. On 2 Jan 2025 it revised this to two destroyed and a third damaged [src:gur-2024-mi8][src:eurosd-2025-magura-mi8-kills]. The video shows one hit and crash [src:aviationist-2024-durso-magura-mi8]. Oryx lists one Mi-8 destroyed with evidence posted on the morning of 31 Dec 2024. That match is our inference from the tweet date [src:oryx-russian-equipment-losses].
  - *2 May 2025.* HUR, the Russian milblogger Rybar and other Russian Telegram channels agree that one Su-30SM went down; its crew was rescued by a civilian ship. Budanov claims a second, with its crew killed, but there is no video [src:twz-2025-altman-su30-aim9][src:maritimeexec-2025-drone-boat-fighter].
  - **Win band:** one to two of each type.
- **Strategic result:** the fleet is based at Novorossiysk and has stayed out of the western Black Sea since late 2023 [src:pravda-2023-mt-anderson-bsf-novorossiysk][src:maritimeexec-2024-bsf-new-measures][src:isw-2024-02-14-roca]. The Ukrainian corridor carried 45 Mt from Greater Odesa in about 9 months (to May 2024), and April 2024 exports were the highest since Feb 2022 [src:pravda-2024-corridor-45mt][src:osw-2024-record-exports].
- **Contested totals** (debrief):

From winter 2023–24, Russia did four things [src:pravda-2024-kyrylenko-krynky-untold]:
- massed glide bombs, FPV drones and fresh assault units (the new 104th VDV Division among them);
- learned to hit the boats at river bends and at the landing points;
- flattened the village;
- made the crossing a "one-way trip" for many [src:slidstvo-2024-stanko-krynky-hell].

- **Anti-USV forces** [src:navalnews-2023-sutton-russia-adapts][src:twz-2024-altman-fpv-helicopters][src:isw-2024-12-31-roca][src:euromaidan-2024-mukhina-sea-baby-machine-guns][src:militarnyi-2025-mi24-raised][src:pravda-2024-kunikov-sunk]:
  - Be-12 flying boats as spotters;
  - dedicated Mi-8 and Ka-27 units with rockets and machine guns, plus Ka-29 gunships;
  - Mi-24VPs brought in from the Baltic Fleet's 396th Regiment;
  - Su-30SM patrols and strafing;
  - Raptor patrol boats and KS-701 Tunets boats;
  - shipborne AK-630M and machine guns;
  - EW jamming;
  - FPVs launched from boats and later from helicopters.
- **How the posture changed:**
  - Oct 2023: ships move to Novorossiysk;
  - Mar 2024: "no ships at sea for two weeks" (Ryzhenko) and operations limited to the eastern sea (UK DI);
  - 2024–25: helicopters and jets hunt drones far offshore [src:pravda-2023-mt-anderson-bsf-novorossiysk][src:hromadske-2024-ryzhenko-bsf][src:maritimeexec-2024-bsf-new-measures][src:isw-2024-12-31-roca].

- **The fleet overall, several baselines:**
  - start of 2025: 58 Tu-22M, 47 Tu-95 and 15 Tu-160 [src:kyivindependent-2025-york-spiderweb];
  - Ihnat (Ukrainian Air Force): 36 of 57 Tu-95 and 10–12 of 19 Tu-160 operational [src:isw-2025-06-03-roca];
  - TWZ: under 50 Tu-95MS and about 60 Tu-22M3 at the start of the war, and about 16 Tu-160 [src:twz-2025-newdick-bomber-force-meaning];
  - The Economist: under 90 operational Tu-22, Tu-95 and Tu-160 together [src:isw-2025-06-01-roca].
- **A-50s:**
  - best case seven in service [src:twz-2025-newdick-bomber-force-meaning];
  - seven operational per the IISS [src:telegraph-2025-barnes-a50-spy-planes];
  - "as few as four" after the strike, per the BBC [src:bbc-2025-adams-turning-point].
- **Base defence:**
  - SAMs, EW and guard patrols with small arms, per Butusov [src:texty-2025-butusov-pavutyna-preparation];
  - Pantsir and S-300 were useless at such short range, per FighterBomber [src:istories-2025-airfield-attacks-main];
  - satellite-navigation jamming did not stop drones controlled over mobile data, per Russian transport-technology analyst Rogozin [src:istories-2025-airfield-attacks-main].

**Places** (OSM [src:osm-2026-stepove-geodata]):
- Stepove 48.196, 37.679;
- Berdychi 48.196, 37.646 (2.4 km west);
- the Avdiivka coke plant 48.173, 37.700 (about 3 km south-east);
- central Avdiivka 48.134, 37.747 (about 9 km south-east);
- Novobakhmutivka 48.249, 37.789 and Novokalynove 48.247, 37.698, to the north.

- **Attribution and framing.** Russia called each attack terrorism against civilian infrastructure. Putin said Ukrainian special services "devised, carried out and ordered" Act 1 [src:wikipedia-2022-crimean-bridge-explosion]. Russia blamed HUR and Budanov, and in Apr 2023 a Moscow court issued an arrest warrant for Budanov [src:pravda-2022-fsb-detains-eight; wikipedia-2022-crimean-bridge-explosion].
- **Investigation (Act 1).** In the Russian version the charge was about 10 t TNT equivalent of solid rocket fuel in 22.7 t of film reels, triggered by GPS at km 156 [src:pravda-2024-kommersant-rocket-fuel]. Twelve people were identified and eight detained in Oct 2022 [src:pravda-2022-fsb-detains-eight]. Eight were jailed for life in Nov 2025 after a closed trial; all denied knowing what the cargo was [src:kyivpost-2025-life-sentences]. Malyuk called the 22 people Russia jailed "ordinary Russian smugglers" [src:militarnyi-2023-kushnikov-malyuk-details].
- **Damage minimised.** Meduza reported a Kremlin guide to the media on playing down the 2022 damage [src:isw-2022-10-08-roca]. The Ministry of Transport claimed the 2023 strike left the rail bridge and road supports intact [src:isw-2023-07-17-roca]. After Act 3, Peskov said "no damage" [src:kyivindependent-2025-fornusek-kremlin-no-damage].
- **Interception claims.** Russia claimed:
  - S-200s downed on 12 Aug 2023, with the smoke presented as a deliberate screen [src:euromaidan-2023-shandra-bridge-smoke; pravda-2023-bridge-smoke-air-defence];
  - three USVs destroyed on 1–2 Sep 2023 [src:aljazeera-2023-sea-drones-bridge];
  - 12 ATACMS and five USVs (by helicopter) on 15 Aug 2024 [src:rferl-2024-kerch-atacms-claim];
  - a drone shot down over the bridge on 3 Jun 2025 [src:kyivindependent-2025-fornusek-underwater].

**Tactical role.**
- Positions and reserves. Treelines hold forward trenches, while reserves, artillery and logistics sit 8–15 km back in treelines, woods and towns [src:cepa-2023-kallberg-treelines].
- Infiltration corridors. The Ukrainian soldier-analyst Hlib Parfonov bases his 2026 report on Russian tactical guidance and his own front-line service. It finds the *longitudinal* attack to be "the predominant method in current practice, employed by both Russian and Ukrainian forces": troops enter a belt from its flank and fight along its interior. The *perpendicular* attack across the open field is "operationally unviable in the majority of cases" because of mines, anti-tank weapons, artillery and exposure [src:saratoga-2026-parfonov-treelines]. The assault group is up to platoon strength and fights in depth along the belt:
  - scouts 7–9 m apart across the belt's width;
  - the main body up to 30 m behind;
  - a consolidation group 100–150 m further back;
  - a reserve up to 450 m from the point.

Operation Spiderweb (Ukrainian *Pavutyna*) was a covert strike by the Security Service of Ukraine (SBU) on Russian long-range aviation at its home bases [src:wikipedia-operation-spiderweb]. Zelensky said it took "one year, six months, and nine days from the start of planning to effective execution" and that he supervised it, while SBU head Lt. Gen. Vasyl Maliuk and his staff carried it out [src:twz-2025-altman-rogoway-bombers-destroyed]. The method:
- the SBU smuggled FPV quadcopters into Russia, and later the mobile wooden cabins [src:bbc-2025-adams-spiders-web-strike];
- inside Russia the drones were hidden under the cabins' roofs, and the cabins were loaded on trucks [src:kyivindependent-2025-york-sbu-bombers-burning];
- Russian drivers who thought they were hauling prefabricated houses drove the trucks to car parks and petrol stations a few kilometres from the bases [src:euromaidanpress-2025-tril-spiderweb] [src:kyivpost-2025-brown-manhunt-drivers];
- the roofs opened remotely and the drones took off [src:guardian-2025-mazhulin-visual-guide].

**Distances.** These are computed great circles between the OSM points above, as a scale for the map, not a firing range. From Robotyne: Kyrylivka 124 km, Obytichna Spit 105 km, Strilkove 186 km, Primorsko-Akhtarsk 237 km, Trudovaya Armeniya 269 km, Taganrog-Yuzhny 230 km, Anapa 295 km. From Krynky: Strilkove 164 km, Kyrylivka 177 km. Akhtubinsk to Primorsko-Akhtarsk is about 660 km. The front itself lay some kilometres south of Robotyne, so true ranges were somewhat shorter. Reported engagement ranges vary:
- **14 Jan:** the A-50 was 160+ km and the Il-22 270+ km from the front [src:mwi-2024-mitchell-awacs-azov]; Kyiv Post later gave 170 km for the first A-50 [src:kyivpost-2024-tuzov-s200]. Defense Express's model puts a "wandering Patriot" 15–25 km behind the front with a 160 km GEM-T [src:espreso-2024-defenseexpress-wandering-patriot].
- **23 Feb**, in ascending order:
  - about 170 km from the front (The Times, as summarised) [src:babel-2024-perepechko-times-a50];
  - over 200 km from the launcher (UP sources in HUR, as reported) [src:kyivpost-2024-tuzov-s200];
  - about 220 km from the nearest Ukrainian positions [src:twz-2024-newdick-second-a50];
  - about 300 km from Ukraine (the Russian court, as reported) [src:kyivpost-2024-dzyaman-court].

November 2023 was the wettest month, with about 154 mm. The soldiers' own accounts:
- winter was "a very difficult time for the operation" [src:slidstvo-2024-stanko-krynky-hell];
- an officer says the withdrawal should have come "in spring at the latest, during the foggy season" [src:pravda-2024-kyrylenko-krynky-untold];
- in summer heat, incendiary drone drops set fires that forced moves [src:kyivindependent-2024-hodunova-krynky-new-positions];
- the 810th Naval Infantry reportedly cited "bad weather conditions" when it refused an assault in late November [src:kyivpost-2023-korshak-glide-bombs-krynky].

The climax came in Feb – Mar 2024, when HUR's Group 13 used night swarms of Magura V5s to sink three ships in five weeks:
- the corvette *Ivanovets*, off Donuzlav on 1 Feb;
- the landing ship *Tsezar Kunikov*, off Alupka on 14 Feb;
- the patrol ship *Sergey Kotov*, off the Kerch Strait on 5 Mar.

**The towns: mining-town geometry.**
- *Footprints.* In OSM, Pokrovsk's town boundary spans about 8.6 × 7.3 km and Myrnohrad's about 5.1 × 8.5 km [src:osm-photon-2026-pokrovsk-places]. Syrskyi put the city at 29 km² [src:united24-2025-litnarovych-155000].
- *Building types.* Both are coal towns that pair mine shafts and spoil heaps (terykony) with apartment blocks and districts of private houses [src:wikipedia-battle-of-pokrovsk]. The Donetsk railway cuts east–west through central Pokrovsk and became the front line inside the city [src:euromaidanpress-2025-mukhina-20-bodies] [src:euromaidanpress-2025-axe-russian-bmp].
- *Mines and heaps in OSM* [src:osm-photon-2026-pokrovsk-places]:
  - the Tsentralna mine (48.312, 37.263) and the spoil heaps of mine No. 3 (48.294, 37.272) and of Tsentralna (48.335, 37.276), in Myrnohrad;
  - the Rodynska mine (48.355, 37.222) at Rodynske;
  - the Kapitalna mine and the Myrnohradska washery (48.329, 37.308) to the east;
  - the Pokrovske mine administration (48.259, 37.061) near Kotlyne;
  - the heap of mine No. 21 (48.257, 37.150) on Pokrovsk's southern edge.
- *Why the heaps matter.* They give fire control over the approaches: Russian forces worked to bypass Selydove's slag heaps, whose capture would give fire control over Pokrovsk's surroundings [src:wikipedia-battle-of-pokrovsk]. On 14 November Russians planted a flag on a ruined mine on Myrnohrad's south-eastern edge [src:euromaidanpress-2025-mukhina-flag-on-mine]. In March 2026 Russian units hung antennas and repeaters on Myrnohrad's high-rises, and those emitters drew Ukrainian strikes [src:isw-2026-03-10-roca].
- *The two towns differ.* Pokrovsk is easy to reach from the south through villages such as Zvirove, where infiltrators use the ruins. Myrnohrad sits among fields with clearer lines of sight [src:euromaidanpress-2025-rfu-black-hawk-corridor].
- *The pocket.* By 29 October the gap between the Russian pincers north of Pokrovsk and south-west of Krasnyi Lyman was about 3 km [src:kyivindependent-2025-farrell-encirclement-what-we-know], and by late November about 1 km [src:euromaidanpress-2025-axe-raiders-425th].

- **The pipe is a hidden, moving spawn.** It is a 10.5 km underground edge with a transit of about four days, its exits shifting [src:meduza-2025-crawl-of-duty] [src:osm-2026-kupiansk-pipeline-geodata]. The player's ISR (drones, intercepts, locals) uncovers exits, and fire control over an exit drives its throughput towards zero. Destroying the pipe should be hard: most attempts failed [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours].
- **There are two routes in.** Closing the pipe is not enough, because the Dvorichna boat route must also be policed and, historically, it was never closed [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours].
- **The win band.**
  - Kupiansk at least 90% Ukrainian by end-December 2025 and the flag on the council by 12 January [src:euromaidan-2026-zoria-kupiansk-pipeline] [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours];
  - north-side villages (Radkivka, Kindrashivka) retaken [src:isw-2025-12-12-roca];
  - about 305 km² swing in late December [src:isw-2026-02-15-roca];
  - the Russian bridgehead at Dvorichna surviving [src:isw-2026-09-26-assessment];
  - Russian losses in the low thousands, the Ukrainian claim being about 2,000 [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours].
- **The information layer.** Russian claims (the 20 Nov "liberation") and Ukrainian silence are a mechanic. Publicising early helps morale but tips off the enemy [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours].
- **Urban fog.** Infiltrators sit in groups of 2–6 among civilians and in civilian clothes. Clearing is slow and sector-based, and radio emissions give men away [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours] [src:isw-2025-09-15-roca].
- **Pacing.** Script one tick per day from late Aug 2025 to 12 Jan 2026, with weather events for foliage cover (Sep–Oct), rain (27 Oct) and fog (about 20 Nov) [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours] [src:isw-2025-11-21-roca].
- **Precedent tutorials.** Avdiivka (Jan 2024) and Sudzha (Mar 2025) can teach the same mechanic in miniature [src:lwj-2024-hardie-avdiivka-analysis] [src:twz-2025-newdick-kursk-pipeline].

  Russian launches follow the Air Force alert log (§2). Each alert is a spawn event with an axis (north, north-east, east, south-east, south-west) [src:uaf-2026-kpszsu-alerts-1-2-july].
- **Win band** (reproduce history, do not beat it):
  - about 92% of all weapons downed or suppressed;
  - about 96% of drones, about 94–100% of cruise missiles;
  - about 14% (4 of 28) of ballistic missiles;
  - 25 ballistic and 12 drone hits on 33 sites;
  - 30–31 dead in Kyiv.

- **2022.** Drones were mostly scouts: commercial quadcopters, Ukrainian fixed-wing ISR (intelligence, surveillance and reconnaissance) and a few Bayraktar TB2s. Around 90% of Ukrainian UAVs were lost, and only about a third of missions succeeded [src:rusi-2022-zabrodskyi-preliminary-lessons]. The Shahed campaign against cities began in late September [src:kaggle-2026-ivaniuk-missile-attacks].
- **2023.** Russia's Orlan-10 plus artillery "reconnaissance-fires circuit" and the Lancet loitering munition dominated; by autumn Lancets were hitting jets about 65 km from Russian-held ground [src:kyivindependent-2023-farrell-lancet]. Ukraine was losing about 10,000 UAVs a month to Russian electronic warfare (EW) [src:rusi-2023-watling-meatgrinder].
- **2024.** The FPV (first-person-view kamikaze quadcopter) boom arrived. RUSI's fieldwork found tactical UAVs caused 60–70% of damaged or destroyed Russian systems, even though 60–80% of FPVs never reached their target [src:rusi-2025-watling-reynolds-third-year]. Shahed-type launches roughly tripled on 2023, to about 11,000, most of them after August [src:kaggle-2026-ivaniuk-missile-attacks].
- **2025.** A "kill zone" 10–15 km deep formed on both sides of the line [src:rbc-2026-fedorov-drone-line][src:osw-2025-game-of-drones]. Several things drove it:
  - the Drone Line project, announced in February [src:kyivindependent-2025-goncharova-drone-line-launch];
  - fiber-optic FPVs with 40+ km reach [src:ukrinform-2025-fedorov-fiber-optic-40km];
  - Russia's Rubikon center hunting Ukrainian drone teams and logistics [src:rferl-2025-rubicon];
  - over 50,000 Shahed-type drones launched at Ukraine in the year [src:isis-2026-anokhin-shahed-2025];
  - mass-produced interceptor drones, at about 950 a day by December [src:kmu-2025-950-interceptors].
- **2026.** Three things changed the picture:
  - Russia lost Starlink in early February (1 Feb per ISW, 5 Feb per Kyiv Independent) [src:isw-2026-feb-26-assessment][src:kyivindependent-2026-starlink-catastrophe]. This grounded its deeper-reaching strike drones [src:euromaidan-2026-axe-starlink-counterattacks], but not its fiber-optic FPVs, so the close kill zone kept growing, to 20–25 km in some sectors by July [src:euromaidanpress-2026-vivdych-kill-zone-corps].
  - Ukrainian ground robots (UGVs) took over a large share of front-line logistics [src:united24-2026-ugv-missions].
  - Russia moved to jet-powered Gerans, which are much harder to intercept [src:united24-2026-place-jet-gerans].

**The pipeline.** It is the Shebelynka–Ostrohozhsk main gas pipeline, a Soviet-built line for gas between Kharkiv Oblast and Russia that was cut off during the 2009 gas war [src:ukrpravda-2026-kyrylenko-kupiansk-is-yours]. Near Kupiansk four abandoned pipelines merge into two across the Oskil, with a diameter of 1 to 1.2 m [src:kyivindependent-2026-terajima-kupiansk-pipelines]. Lengths vary with the endpoints chosen:
- "a walk of about 10 km in darkness" that exits in a forest [src:euromaidan-2026-zoria-kupiansk-pipeline];
- "more than 10 km" for the civilians led through it [src:kyivpost-2026-khartiia-pipeline-civilians];
- 15 km from the Russian rear to Ukrainian lines [src:kyivindependent-2026-terajima-kupiansk-pipelines];
- 10.5 km by our own computation (method below).

**What countered it, or might have.**
- **On the day:** the only direct action was civilians throwing stones [src:wikipedia-operation-spiderweb]. The tyres on the wings did not save the aircraft, and some observers mistook them for scorch marks [src:twz-2025-newdick-confirmed-losses].
- **Afterwards, as analysts advise or Russia did:**
  - truck inspections [src:twz-2025-altman-what-we-know-spiderweb];
  - mobile air-defence groups and wider coverage [src:isw-2025-06-01-roca];
  - control of mobile communications and network traffic around strategic sites (Rogozin) [src:istories-2025-airfield-attacks-main];
  - dispersal [src:moscowtimes-2025-bombers-relocated];
  - hardened shelters [src:twz-2026-newdick-bomber-shelters].
- **Lutsenko's view:** an exploit this good is hard to repeat [src:texty-2025-butusov-pavutyna-preparation].

**The Russian package.**
- **Everything at once.** The Air Force's "distinctive feature": many weapon types used simultaneously from many directions, with many ballistic missiles and jet drones [src:uaf-2026-kpszsu-alerts-1-2-july]. Aviation analyst Dolintsev says using many weapon types in the same time window wears down the defence and makes the problem "exceptionally complex" [src:bbc-2026-rainsford-most-massive-kyiv].
- **The sequence**, about 16 hours long:
  1. a drone and jet-drone prelude from about 19:30;
  2. a ballistic salvo from about 01:50 to 02:30 (with Zircons);
  3. cruise missiles from about 02:50 to 03:50;
  4. Banderols and jet drones until about 11:00.

- **Siege by infrastructure destruction.** Strikes on water, power and heat were meant to force capitulation without assault [src:isw-2022-03-02-roca]. HRW surveyed 4,884 damaged or destroyed buildings over 14 km², including 93% of high-rises [src:hrw-2024-beneath-the-rubble].
- **Heavy air bombardment of the plant.** Tu-22M3s [src:isw-2022-04-17-roca], multi-ton "bunker-busters" on the field hospital [src:isw-2022-04-28-roca], 38 strikes and 4 bomber flights in a single day [src:isw-2022-05-11-roca] and unguided "dumb bombs" [src:isw-2022-05-10-roca]. **FAB-3000:** there were Telegram photos of a FAB-3000 at a Tu-22M (6 Apr) and a Ukrainian warning of its use (16 Apr) [src:aerotime-2022-petrauskaite-fab-3000] [src:infobae-2022-fab-3000-mariupol]. Use is reported, not confirmed in the sources fetched here.
- **Other fires.** Thermobaric weapons [src:isw-2022-03-30-roca], naval gunfire [src:isw-2022-05-02-roca] [src:isw-2022-05-15-roca], thermite incendiaries [src:isw-2022-05-15-roca] and ATGMs against vehicles and people [src:ukrinform-2022-azov-atgm-evacuation-vehicle] [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters].
- **Ukrainian defence in depth underground.** Shelters and tunnels let the garrison survive bombardment and shift between positions [src:twz-2022-altman-flint-helicopter-rescue]. Russia answered by blockading the tunnel exits rather than clearing them [src:isw-2022-05-12-roca], and by assaults from the north and later along the M14 at the western entrance [src:isw-2022-04-19-roca] [src:isw-2022-05-13-roca].
- **The Mi-8 air bridge.** The profile was:
  - nap-of-the-earth flight at 10–15 m under night-vision goggles, 3 m in places [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters] [src:abc-2022-ap-mariupol-rescue-missions];
  - about 150 mph (220 km/h) [src:twz-2022-altman-flint-helicopter-rescue] [src:abc-2022-ap-mariupol-rescue-missions];
  - about 80 min each way, 42 of them over enemy ground [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters];
  - a refuelling point at the line on the way back, and fuel carried only to that point, to save weight [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters];
  - artillery fired a distraction at the planned front-line crossing [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters];
  - 10 minutes on the ground with engines running (12 and 15–20 minutes in two accounts) [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters] [src:twz-2022-altman-flint-helicopter-rescue];
  - an intelligence officer aboard each helicopter, and crews in civilian clothes to escape on foot if shot down [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters] [src:twz-2022-altman-flint-helicopter-rescue];
  - routes alternated between land and sea, once passing between two Russian ships [src:twz-2022-altman-flint-helicopter-rescue].
  Threats were Pantsir-S1 (briefed, no Buk), ship fire, a missile that failed to explode and MANPADS [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters] [src:abc-2022-ap-mariupol-rescue-missions] [src:rbc-2026-honcharova-serbov-interview]. Ukrainian counters were the Mi-24 decoy (which failed on mission 6), surprise, and never sending the same pilot twice [src:time-2024-spencer-collins-mariupol-helicopters]. Russia's counter was to thicken air defences once the flights became public, which ended the missions [src:twz-2022-altman-mariupol-helicopter-missions].
- **Information.** With no internet, the defenders printed A4 leaflets with front news to keep up morale [src:pravda-2025-palamar-mariupol-interview]. Outbound media (GoPro clips, Kozatskyi's photos) mattered politically [src:rbc-2026-honcharova-serbov-interview] [src:kyivindependent-2024-shulzhenko-kozatskyi].

Ukraine then halted the main advance on the Oskil, around 11–12 September, and widened the gains piecemeal:
- a Borova bridgehead on 13 September;
- the east bank of Kupiansk on 16 September;
- Kupiansk-Vuzlovyi on 26 September;
- a pincer on Lyman, which fell on 1 October, the day after Putin's annexation ceremony.

- **Visibility is a gradient, not fog of war.** Model detection probability as a function of distance from the line and sensor density:
  - near-certain within about 3 km;
  - falling off to 15–25 km;
  - "tasked only" beyond 40 km, with satellites cueing longer-range drones.

